Statements:
[
    "Harness is the engineering layer around the model (the “brain”), not the hardware or the model itself.",
    "It’s the software glue that decides how the model is used, what it can remember, and which tools it can call.",
    "Memory handling determines what data to store (short-term vs long-term memory), how it’s indexed, retrieval methods, forgetting/decay policies, privacy and data governance.",
    "Tooling and plugins define how the model calls external services (calculators, databases, search, file I/O, code execution), how tool calls are formatted, how failures are handled, and how results are integrated back into prompts.",
    "Context and prompts management covers system prompts, dynamic prompts, context injection (including memory content and tool results), and multi-turn workflow orchestration.",
    "Orchestration and routing involves selecting which model to use, failover and model routing if one model is banned or slow, load balancing, and versioning.",
    "Safety and governance encompass guardrails, content filters, auditing, logging, and compliance checks.",
    "Deployment concerns include configuration-driven behavior, easy swapping of models, monitoring, and performance tuning.",
    "Why this matters for sovereignty: changing the harness can change how it behaves, what it remembers, and which tools it uses.",
    "If you switch brains, you often have to re-tune the harness (memory policies, prompts, tool adapters) to maintain performance.",
    "Example: moving from Claude Code to OpenCode would keep the same harness structure but adjust the tool adapters and prompts to accommodate differences in the model’s behavior, so memory usage and tool calls still produce the same outcomes.",
    "If you’d like, I can provide a concise checklist for building a sovereign AI harness focused on memory and tool handling."
] 

